WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
How can you test developing human brain tissue inside a lab?

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.740
You cannot experiment on a growing human brain.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.700
Instead, researchers grow living mini-organs, called organoids, inside a dish.

4
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:18.653
Start with a patient's skin cell.

5
00:00:19.893 --> 00:00:23.713
A few proteins called transcription factors reset its identity.

6
00:00:25.053 --> 00:00:28.893
It becomes an induced pluripotent stem cell, or iPS cell.

7
00:00:30.253 --> 00:00:33.453
Pluripotent means it can make every cell type of the body.

8
00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:36.593
And it carries that patient's own genome.

9
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:40.783
Next, let those stem cells clump and float in the dish.

10
00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:46.003
No one places them, yet they sort themselves by self-organization.

11
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:51.123
They form a polarized sheet with a definite inside and outside.

12
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:57.143
Dividing precursors called progenitors line a fluid-filled cavity, while young

13
00:00:57.143 --> 00:00:58.603
neurons lie further out.

14
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:04.333
Other organoids, like mini-intestines, start from adult tissue stem cells.

15
00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:09.373
They are multipotent, making several cell types within one tissue.

16
00:01:10.673 --> 00:01:16.313
In 2009, Toshiro Sato and colleagues grew them in Matrigel, a basement-membrane

17
00:01:16.313 --> 00:01:21.393
matrix. They added growth factors that keep the stem cells dividing.

18
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:28.373
Here is the rule: stem cells self-organize into mini-organs that model tissue, never

19
00:01:28.373 --> 00:01:29.373
whole bodies.

20
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:32.037
Now pause and test that rule.

21
00:01:33.677 --> 00:01:36.817
Suppose a virus stunts a human brain organoid in your dish.

22
00:01:37.877 --> 00:01:41.117
Or sugar makes one donor's gut organoid release glutamate.

23
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:46.297
What do those experiments prove, and what can they never show on their own?

24
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:56.277
Organoids show which human cells a virus harms, not pregnancy risk.

25
00:01:57.597 --> 00:02:02.917
An old lab virus strain can mislead, so researchers retest epidemic strains in

26
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:04.497
organoids and pregnant mice.

27
00:02:05.877 --> 00:02:11.277
In 2022, Buchanan and colleagues saw glutamate release from one donor's gut

28
00:02:11.277 --> 00:02:17.957
organoids. That suggests a conserved response, not human behaviour, so checks in

29
00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:19.337
native tissue remain vital.

30
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.190
Used with those guards, this method unlocked three discoveries.

31
00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:31.890
First, in 2008, Mototsugu Eiraku, Yoshiki Sasai and colleagues floated mouse and

32
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.590
human embryonic stem cells.

33
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.690
Embryonic stem cells are pluripotent cells from an early embryo.

34
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:45.030
Their clumps built cortical sheets with a definite inside and outside, with no one

35
00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.030
placing a cell.

36
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:51.190
Second, organoids pinpointed which human cells Zika virus harms.

37
00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:58.710
In March 2016, Guo-li Ming, Hongjun Song and Hengli Tang tracked Zika in human

38
00:02:58.710 --> 00:02:59.830
cortical progenitors.

39
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.690
It infected progenitors far more readily than stem cells or immature neurons,

40
00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.090
killing some and slowing growth.

41
00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:13.270
Stevens Rehen's group showed Zika stunts human brain organoids.

42
00:03:14.233 --> 00:03:18.053
Third, organoids exposed cells too scarce to study alone.

43
00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:23.133
Enterochromaffin cells are under 1 percent of the gut lining.

44
00:03:24.273 --> 00:03:27.513
Yet they make more than 90 percent of the body's serotonin.

45
00:03:28.913 --> 00:03:33.953
In 2017, Bellono and colleagues genetically tagged them in mouse intestinal

46
00:03:33.953 --> 00:03:36.513
organoids and checked native tissue.

47
00:03:37.793 --> 00:03:42.693
Across organs, mini-organs reveal tissue rules without replacing a whole body.
